From 8f8c2626ebe6b6a6e6e6e965187e7014dfc0fee3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 23:09:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/44] dns: take the provider's DNS from netifd for the output
 device, ask the fastest

The fallback read the first three servers of resolv.conf.auto, where
OpenWrt lists the DNS servers of every interface that is up, sorted by
metrics and names: a VPN tunnel, a LAN with DNS set by hand or a backup
modem often come before the WAN. Behind the socket that podkop binds to
the WAN these cannot answer: they ate the time of the fallback or pushed
the WAN servers out of the three.

Take the servers of the interfaces on the output device of sing-box (the
device of auto_detect_interface or default_interface, else of the default
route) from netifd: `ubus call network.interface dump`, their l3_device
and dns-server. For DHCP or PPPoE these are wan and wan6 (or wan_6);
other devices are left out, and so are addresses of the router itself,
as dnsmasq does. The list is read at the first fallback, and again when
sing-box resets the network after its default interface changed (the DNS
router's ResetNetwork calls ResetFallbackProviders), after all its
servers missed, and 30 s after ubus could not be asked. The read runs
outside the path of queries, one at a time.

Pick the server as dnsmasq does: the first fallback query goes to all of
them at once (at most 4), the first answer wins and gets the next queries
alone, and all are asked again every 20 s or 50 queries and as soon as it
misses one.

Tested with podkop 0.7.22 on OpenWrt 24.10 and 25.12, 19 scenarios, with
a tunnel and a LAN listing DNS before the WAN and a dead first WAN
server: only the WAN servers are taken, the dead one is asked once and
the living one answers the rest; when the WAN goes down and up with other
servers, the next fallback takes them.
